Version 1 sync calls to third parties

Every request makes a sync HTTP call to the auth server
MeasurementsService calls authService.getUser(token) on every endpoint. That's an HTTP round-trip per request, blocking a Tomcat thread until the auth server responds. Under load, this serializes throughput.

 WebClient.block() is the worst of both worlds
AuthService uses reactive WebClient but calls .block() on every result. You pay the reactive machinery overhead without getting non-blocking behavior, and you're blocking Tomcat threads on Netty's event loop work.

Sequential auth + Influx calls in MeasurementsService
getMeasurements/getFields/getData do auth-check then Influx query, both blocking. If you keep blocking I/O, parallelizing them with CompletableFuture cuts latency roughly in half — though it also wastes Influx work when auth fails. With caching from #2, this becomes mostly irrelevant.

Version 3 virtual threads
1. The ioExecutor went from bounded to effectively unbounded
project/backend/business-logic/src/main/java/com/example/demo/Configuration/ExecutorConfig.java
threads-right-way:
ThreadPoolTaskExecutor: core=16, max=64, queue=200, CallerRunsPolicy
Caps concurrency at 64 and applies backpressure (CallerRunsPolicy runs work on the caller when the queue fills).
virtual-threads:
Executors.newThreadPerTaskExecutor(Thread.ofVirtual().name("io-vt-", 0).factory())
Spawns a fresh virtual thread per task. There is no cap, no queue, no rejection policy.
Under load every incoming request fans out two supplyAsync(..., ioExecutor) calls (auth + influx), and now each one gets its own brand-new virtual thread. So if 500 requests are in flight, you have ~1000 simultaneous downstream calls instead of the 64 + 200-queue you had before.
2. The bottleneck just moved — and got worse
The InfluxDB Java client uses OkHttp under the hood, which has a Dispatcher with a default maxRequestsPerHost=5. When 1000 virtual threads try to query Influx, ~5 actually run and the rest sit in the dispatcher queue. Same story for Reactor Netty's connection pool driving the WebClient calls to Auth. The "virtualness" of the threads doesn't help — the contention point is the HTTP client's connection pool, and now you've stacked far more work onto it than before.
Meanwhile Influx itself has a finite query-execution capacity. With backpressure gone, queries pile up server-side and tail latency explodes.
3. Rate limiter on Auth starts firing 429s
project/backend/Auth/src/main/java/org/example/auth/config/RateLimitFilter.java is still per-user token-bucket. Every business-logic request triggers an /auth/me call. With unbounded fan-out, you hit the limit and AuthService.getUser throws InvalidTokenException("Too many requests to auth server") — which then turns successful work into thrown exceptions. The new metrics.recordAuthClientError("me", "RateLimited") counter will show this clearly.
4. WebClient.block() on a virtual thread can pin the carrier
project/backend/business-logic/src/main/java/com/example/demo/auth/AuthService.java still uses authClient.post()...block(). With spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true, the calling thread is a virtual thread. That's legal, but any synchronized block held during the wait (Reactor has a few, OkHttp has more) pins the virtual thread to its carrier — and you have as many carriers as CPU cores. A few pinned carriers under load = head-of-line blocking that simply cannot happen on the platform-thread version.
You can confirm with -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=full on the JVM.
5. BCrypt + virtual threads is a bad combo
Auth's passwordEncoder.matches(...) is CPU-bound BCrypt. Virtual threads buy you nothing on CPU-bound work — and while BCrypt is running it holds the carrier. With spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true you now have only ~N-cores carriers handling everything, and BCrypt logins eat them.
6. Metric overhead grew, but it's small
The new publishPercentileHistogram + SLO buckets on http.server.requests, http.client.requests, and every custom timer adds HdrHistograms per meter. Real but a few percent at most — not the cause of "plummeted."
What to try
1.
Cap the OkHttp/Influx dispatcher — bump maxRequestsPerHost (try 32–64) and maxRequests on the InfluxDB client's OkHttpClient.Builder. Without this, virtual threads can't help you.
2.
Reintroduce backpressure on the ioExecutor. Either wrap the virtual-thread factory with a Semaphore (acquire before submit) sized to your downstream capacity, or use a SemaphoreBoundedExecutor pattern. Pure thread-per-task on Influx-backed code is rarely what you want.
3.
Don't put BCrypt on virtual threads. Run the password-hashing on a small bounded platform-thread pool, or accept the CPU bottleneck explicitly.
4.
Turn on -Djdk.tracePinnedThreads=short during the perf test — if you see pinning under load, that's a separate, real problem.
5.
Check Auth 429 counter (auth.rate_limit.hits or whatever the metric ended up named) during the failing run. If it's elevated, raise rate-limit.requests for the perf test or you're measuring rate-limiter behavior, not throughput.
6.
Compare under matched concurrency. If the perf test is open-loop, virtual threads will accept more in-flight requests and Little's Law guarantees worse latency. Pin the test to the same concurrency level for an apples-to-apples comparison.
The short version: you replaced a bounded pool with an unbounded one and disabled your only backpressure mechanism. Virtual threads aren't slower — they just stopped protecting your downstreams.